This is what you want to go
for in indeed index-match

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.750
is often contrasted with Vlookup

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.180
and then Vlookup is often
then considered the beginner,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.720
the new version of the formula,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.640
whereas the real pros
are using index-match.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.560
A bit similar to that is
this one here is a Vlookup,

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.100
no thank you, but index-match
is what you need to do.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.203
The next one Excel user index-match,

00:02:27.203 --> 00:02:30.270
Vlookup again, the rivalry
between index-match and Vlookup,

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.340
of course, index-match is what
is considered superior here.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.890
And yes, then the consultants
are able of doing index-match,

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.265
sometimes consider themselves

00:02:38.265 --> 00:02:40.510
of super professional Excel users.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.800
And then finally here just
a comparison of Vlookup

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.270
and index-match, so
Vlookup is super popular,

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.040
whereas index-match is not so much,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.910
and probably the main reason
why Vlookup is so popular

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.960
is because it's much easier to use,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.710
but actually index-match
is much more powerful,

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:59.550
it's much more flexible in
the way how you can use it,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.300
and it's also much more
error prone in many ways.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.170
And also it is faster.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.700
So Excel takes less time to calculate,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.880
and probably this was something
that was even more relevant

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.370
in the past and not so much
with the newer versions

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.896
of Excel, but the
index-match is considered

00:03:14.896 --> 00:03:17.467
a more and better performing
version of a lookup.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:19.270
But now enough with these jokes,

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.310
let's now understand how
you can use index-match.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:23.667
Let's learn how it really works.

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:26.610
And I will contrast it with Vlookup,

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.400
just to make sure that you
understand the differences.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.840
And the business setting,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.700
the business application
that I wanna have here

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.610
is a little small exercise,

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.450
and this is something
that you could easily

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.360
then also be exposed to

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.950
in any financial modeling
type of situation.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.580
Now, here we are facing the situation

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.390
that we are a retail company,
we have a number of stores.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.670
So now I just gave a unique
number to every store.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.310
So these are pretty much in
all the names of the stores.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:57.360
And now each of these stores
had a forecast for the year.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.680
So for the next year,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.250
there is a company plan
and these are the values

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.200
that these stores are supposed to make.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.000
But now you want to break
down these yearly targets

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.080
to the individual monthly values, okay?

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.170
And you wanna do that
by pretty much applying

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:18.770
the historic distribution of
the revenues across the months.

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:21.100
And let's not just assume that somebody

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.370
from the finance department
put together for each

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.060
of these stores, here now you
can see that they are orders.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.570
These are now the exact certain
stores that we saw before.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.000
Here we put together what the
percentage of the revenues are

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.110
that accrue in the individual months,

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.490
based on historic figures.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.170
So how this distribution
was in the prior years.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:45.690
And then you always see that
the total for each line is 100.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.050
So this is always the sum of these values,

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.510
but you can see that the
distribution is quite different.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:51.343
So for instance,

00:04:51.343 --> 00:04:54.653
store number one made 18.8%
of the revenues in January,

00:04:54.653 --> 00:04:56.680
whereas store number two,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.080
made 7% of the revenues in January.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.420
And now we need to do the following.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.760
Mainly we need to match apply this number

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.760
with the corresponding
percentage of the historic split.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.530
And this is something
were you would usually use

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.670
a lookup four.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:19.260
And so what you need here
is a 2-dimensional lookup

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.030
to pretty much picking the
right value for the store

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.020
and for the month.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.300
So 2-dimension lookup.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.740
So let's now start by looking
into how this would work

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.430
with Vlookup and now I
would assume that you have

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.310
an understanding of how Vlookup works.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.320
If you don't, then please just look it up,

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.490
there are many resources on that online.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:41.480
I will later explained
index-match in much more detailed,

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.130
but Vlookup just as a basic comparison.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.280
And what most people will do,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.110
I can tell you from my experience,

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.400
if they would need to
do something like this,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.940
they will type in
Vlookup, they choose here

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.990
the lookup value, then here you fix now

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:59.480
of course the columns.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.310
Then you need to choose
then the table array.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.410
So this is always the first
table here the reference,

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:10.410
the D numbers, and then
here they choose the columns

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.780
that are relevant, here are 13 columns.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.610
And now for January,

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.260
they then put the number
two because this is then

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.590
the second column and then an exact match.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:22.253
And then what you have here,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.230
this is now a percentage value, right?

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.370
But what you have here is 0.09,

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:33.080
which should be the value from
January for store number six.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.170
So let's check store number six.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:36.920
January is 8.7.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.530
So I assume if you just take
one more digit, yeah 8.7,

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:43.710
this is not the percentage
figure that we got earlier.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.460
But now of course the downside is

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.580
even if I now fix this reference here,

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.630
if I want to now have it for
February for the next month,

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.560
if I just copy it over, it doesn't work

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.470
because those two, this
number is not dynamic,

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:59.240
but here for February, I
would need a three, right?

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.890
So the next column in the
historical split table

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.220
to really make use of that.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.040
It's actually what I have seen people do

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.930
was that then they would actually
just manually type in here

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:12.710
a three and here a four and so on

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.800
just to get all the
monthly values correct.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.976
So let's just check here
for six, eight, seven,

00:07:17.976 --> 00:07:22.350
13.2, so this fits, and then here 12.7,

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:23.830
so this fits as well,

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.230
but of course typing
all of this in manually

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.810
of course, isn't really the way to go.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:30.830
No of course not, maybe for the 12 here

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:33.010
it might even work, but now
imagine having to do this

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:36.340
for a much larger table,
with many more columns,

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.280
then it just wouldn't
be able to handle it.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.580
And then what some maybe a
bit smarter people would do

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.887
is that they would include
something like a helper line here

00:07:43.887 --> 00:07:46.870
were then instead of writing the two out,

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.220
they would just link it here

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.700
and then basically do it like this.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.000
And now of course you have
the advantage of being able

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.020
to automate it in some ways.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.710
But now what do you do if
for whatever reason here

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.770
a new column gets inserted?

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.370
Because now this doesn't work anymore

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.360
because now the fifth
column here is empty,

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.762
whereas you want to have the
value for April in the column

00:08:09.762 --> 00:08:10.857
here for April, right?

00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:13.600
And so for all these kinds of reasons,

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:17.160
using Vlookup in the classical setup

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.010
just doesn't make a lot of sense,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.900
and it's just not recommended

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.970
and it's just not really
considered professional.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:27.130
And this is now really where
index-match comes into play.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.350
So how does index-match work?

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.420
And before we now dive
into the numbers here,

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:32.750
let me try to give you a bit more

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:36.110
of an intuitive explanation
into all of this.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.780
So index-match is a
combination of two formulas.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:41.330
It's a combination of the index formula,

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:44.570
which is then combined
with the match formula.

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:47.860
So how do these two
formulas work together?

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.950
And how it works is that
in the index formula,

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:52.360
you define an array.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.860
So basically you define
a range of a table,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.610
so certain reference which
can be somewhere in the middle

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:01.390
of an Excel sheet that
is now the new range

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:03.630
where the formula looks into.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:07.280
And now, so applying this to
this historical split table

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:11.310
that we used, here we had all
the numbers of the stores.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.500
So from one to five and
turn the very end, 30,

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.830
and then on this axis, you
had January, February, March,

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:21.250
April, and so on.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:26.250
And now what the match formula
does is it looks both into

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:30.200
the rows and also into
the columns and saying,

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.200
okay, return to me what
number here is, for instance,

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.200
the store number three.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.690
And now, because this is ordered,

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:42.910
the store number three also
comes into the third position

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:44.270
in this array.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:47.830
And the same is now true
for the second match formula

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:51.137
that we will use to now
pull up the columns,

00:09:51.137 --> 00:09:53.800
and here again now for it
it could look into, okay,

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.800
what number in this array is
actually the month of April.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.930
And here now, the way
we have set it up here,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:05.730
it would be one, two, three, four, right?

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:09.930
And then it can give me
pretty much the number three

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.470
in the rows and the
number four in the columns

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.740
to them put up this exact value.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:16.890
So this is how you use it,

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.450
and I can understand that now
this was a bit hard to follow,

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:24.010
but let's now jump into
the practical example

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:25.420
to really bring this to life.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:29.010
And we now start by setting
up the index formula.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:32.220
And here now this is now the
index for the store number six,

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.070
but first we need to define the array.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:36.880
So the overall table that
now the values are supposed

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:38.020
to be pulled out.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:41.420
And here we now just mark
this data table here.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:46.420
So now I just made a
reference here from A3 to N33,

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:49.470
just to mark this whole
table here and I fix it.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.170
So now we have the whole array defined.

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.580
So this is now the
overall field from rich X

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.520
and I will pull out individual numbers.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.820
And now it asks for a row
number and for a column number.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.320
So how this works is that
now the very first field

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.220
of this array, and here you
see it starts at the field A3.

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:11.480
This now has the row number
one and the column number one.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.670
So this is field one, one.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.090
This one here, one below, this
would be the row number two,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:21.160
because there are two rows
and then still the column one.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:22.550
This field here for instance,

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:27.110
this would be the row
number one, two, three,

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:30.420
and then the column number
one, two, three, four.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.750
So three, four would
give us in this field.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:37.300
So that's not just do an example
for the store six, January

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:38.710
that we just had selected.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:41.120
And now for January,
this would be the number

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.060
that we would want to pull out, right?

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.456
So the 8.7%.

00:11:45.456 --> 00:11:48.800
And this now would pretty
much mean row number of seven.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:51.000
So this is the seventh row, right?

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.010
So one, two, three, four,

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.750
five, six, seven.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.730
So for the row number,
here is asking you for,

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:01.500
we type in the seven and
then for the column number,

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.900
because this is January,
this is the second column,

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:07.050
so we type in here the second column.

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:10.120
And now this is all ready what we have.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.120
And if we now type this in,

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:16.304
then of course we get
exactly this number 8.7%.

00:12:16.304 --> 00:12:19.310
And this was this number
here that we wanted.

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:21.100
So far, so good.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:24.320
But now of course you
want to make this dynamic

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.130
because now you would
have the same problem

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:30.170
as if you would just use the
Vlookup in the prior example,

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:32.420
and what you want to use here instead

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:34.680
are now the match formulas.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.430
So both of these two numbers,

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:39.010
both the column and the row look up,

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:41.760
needs to be replaced by a match formula.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.980
And let's just start
here by the row number.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:46.300
So we pull up the match formula

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.740
and now he looks for a lookup value,

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.900
and in the rows, you remember
that we had the stores.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.480
So we now highlight here the store value,

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:59.480
we fix the columns to be able
to now always look up here

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:01.620
the stores in every field.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:05.460
Now it asks for a lookup
error for the match.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:09.850
And now all you need to
highlight the exact column

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:12.960
from the exact length as
you also did for the array.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.660
So you see regarding the
rows that the rows go

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.330
from three to 33.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.870
So now here is where you need to create

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:24.890
a match lookup array that
has the same kind of size,

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:27.600
namely A3 to A33.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:31.200
And then the match type is zero.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:33.620
So let's now see if
this was done correctly,

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:37.520
because if it did it should
still now give us the 8.7

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:41.075
and it does, right, it
still gives us the 8.7%,

00:13:41.075 --> 00:13:44.767
or Euro because it's formatted
as a Euro value, 0.087.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:49.270
And now let's just check
just to double, triple check

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:50.380
if this is really the case,

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:52.600
if we evaluate this expression

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.480
and you can always do that by pressing F9,

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:57.740
if you press F9 on such a thing,

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:00.700
it returns the value that
then the certain formula

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.000
within a formula is actually returning.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:07.180
So if I press F9 I can see that
this whole match expression

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:09.510
is returning the value seven,

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:13.360
which is exactly the one that
we expected it to return.

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:14.630
So just explain it again.

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:17.000
Why is this returning a seven?

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:20.050
Because the match formula as we had here,

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:23.550
it now gives a certain range
as we now have to find it here

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:27.340
from A3 to A33, this is how we set it up.

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:28.370
So from here to here,

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.750
this is what we gave as a lookup value.

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.530
And now it takes this value here,

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:35.900
this here defined the value six,

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:38.020
it now goes into this range

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:41.460
and it looks for this value six, right?

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:44.850
So it looks in this range
where is the value six?

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:46.777
And now of course it finds it here,

00:14:46.777 --> 00:14:50.330
and now it returns the relative position

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:53.030
of this element in this lookup area.

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:54.910
So now starting at the top,

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:56.810
it counts one, two, three,

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:59.060
four, five, six, seven.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.040
So it says, okay, this
value that I found here,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.070
this value six is the seventh number,

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:07.880
the seventh item in this lookup area.

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:10.080
And this of course now is exactly
what do you want it to do,

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.660
right, because we want to know what number

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.030
it is regarding the rows,

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:17.030
and this is exactly what the
match formula is returning.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:19.490
And as we all ready
know, this is now seven,

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:21.090
which is right, returns a seven.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.830
So now when let's do the
same thing for the columns,

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.800
and again, we use instead of
now manually writing it in,

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:30.550
match for it returning the column figure.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:34.100
And we do that again for us
by providing the lookup value.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:36.810
And now in the columns,
it's the months, right?

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.250
So you wanna look up the
months in the new lookup area.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:44.320
So, you now highlight or
select this January thing,

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.460
and now you fixed the row.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:51.350
And now next you define the
lookup area or the lookup array.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:54.120
And this is now a here,
this one, and again,

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.880
you need to go over the same size exactly

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.310
how your overall index array was set up,

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:03.680
and you'll see that this was
going from column A to column N

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.490
so here you now need
to make sure that this

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.400
is now going from column A to column N

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.450
to really make these arrays match, right?

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:15.800
So that now the first, second,
third, fourth, et cetera,

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:20.530
position of this array is
the same as the number of,

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:22.920
regarding the positions of the columns

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.960
of the overall array that you
defined here for the index.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.960
So let's now fix that as well.

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:31.330
And let's now again, do the match type,

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:35.130
and this is supposed to be
always zero and exact match.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.660
And now, again, it stays
at the same number.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:39.060
So this seems to be correct.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:42.010
And if we not evaluate
this second match formula,

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:43.600
it returns a two.

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.070
And this of course is
exactly what we wanted to do.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:48.060
So again, how does this work?

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:52.770
Here we gave the January
as the lookup value,

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:55.080
then here we defined the array,

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.870
and now it looks into this array

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:00.630
what position starting here from the left

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.477
is this value that he's looking for,

00:17:02.477 --> 00:17:04.830
mainly this Jan, the January value.

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:07.390
And now here, you can actually
see that it's the second one

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:11.240
from the left, so it returns
the number or the index two.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:13.510
Now with these two lookup values,

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.950
pretty much the row index
and the column index,

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:20.650
we can then really look up
exactly this precise percentage

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:21.483
that we were looking for.

00:17:21.483 --> 00:17:24.520
And now of course the
beauty is if we fixed

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.060
all of this correctly, and
I all ready see that here,

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:29.060
now we haven't fix this property,

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:31.670
so let's just check this
is fully fixed here,

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:35.350
the column is fixed, this is
fixed here, the rows are fixed,

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:38.020
and this is fixed, so it should be fine.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:40.283
If we now copy it over.

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:44.710
So it should be now able to directly give

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:47.440
the correct numbers here everywhere.

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:49.110
And this all ready looks quite good.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:54.110
So let's just check by
including some values,

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.800
because now every row should
return the number one.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:00.350
So I just included here
