00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.860
So this is how it works, this
is how you use index-match,

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:36.960
and just going back to the
example that we had earlier,

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:41.500
if we now here start adding
a new column, for instance,

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:43.510
or even several new columns,

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:46.580
all of this should not be bothered at all

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:48.980
because we made this as a reference.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:52.460
Now, again, it includes
automatically all the ranges.

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:56.370
So you can see that instead of
going from A to N as before,

00:19:56.370 --> 00:20:00.940
now, it just goes from A to
P, so just expanded that.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:04.961
And because it doesn't care
about the absolute value here,

00:20:04.961 --> 00:20:08.360
with the match, you just
directly pull up the right index.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:11.060
And of course you could have also included

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:14.360
the match function in your
Vlookup to do that instead.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:15.850
But then if you start doing that,

00:20:15.850 --> 00:20:17.730
then why not directly using index-match

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.060
and get the other benefits

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.030
like the performance
improvements this way.

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:22.970
So hope that this was helpful.

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.700
So the bottom line is that
pretty much when ever you wanna

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:28.830
use a 2-dimensional lookup, in most cases

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:31.790
using an index match is the way to go.

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:33.950
And even with only a
one dimensional lookup,

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:34.900
there can be reasons,

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:37.210
but then you would choose an index-match

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:38.500
over just the Vlookup.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.390
Of course, there are good
reasons to look Vlookup as well,

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:45.010
probably just easier to set
up, just a bit more convenient

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.890
and probably even the biggest
pros are still using Vlookup,

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:51.090
if they just quickly wanna do something,

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.840
even though maybe they just
claim or pretend they don't.

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:55.220
But I mean, it's okay, right.

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:57.740
But just be aware when
you use it and be aware

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:00.690
of the disadvantages and
all that this really helps

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.400
to step up your Excel
skills, because index-match

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:05.049
is something that requires a bit more

00:21:05.049 --> 00:21:07.150
